[FINAL-TEXT] All evidence is in. FDB shows both MACs learned on br_vlan10, ARP is populated both ways, no iptables rules, STP disabled, 0% loss in both ping directions.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Direct and exhaustive investigation of every component in the path between Laptop2 and Laptop3 found no fault. Both devices are on the same Layer-2 domain: Laptop2 (172.16.10.12) connects to switch1 port eth3 and Laptop3 (172.16.10.13) connects to switch1 port eth4. Both ports are correctly assigned to bridge br_vlan10 on switch1, matching the startup script — no VLAN mismatch exists. The forwarding database on switch1 contains dynamically-learned entries for both MACs (32:e9:2b:ce:3a:e3 on eth3, 4a:b8:4a:ca:09:5b on eth4), confirming two-way L2 learning is working. Bidirectional ICMP tests (three packets each direction) completed with 0% loss and normal sub-5ms RTTs. ARP tables on both laptops are populated with each other's MAC address and with the gateway (172.16.10.1). Laptop3 has no iptables rules — all chains are ACCEPT with zero rules. No spanning-tree port blocking is present (STP is disabled on all bridges). The uplink trunk through mswitch1 to router0 is correctly carrying VLAN10 traffic, and the gateway is reachable from both laptops. The reported symptom — bidirectional communication failure between Laptop2 and Laptop3 — cannot be reproduced; the network is fully operational. The outage was most likely a transient event (brief interface flap, ARP or FDB flush at startup) that resolved on its own before this investigation began. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===